WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.222
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:09.048
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.348 --> 00:00:15.832
Unit eleven: Discourse Analysis. Today you will emphasise processes rather than agents with the passive.

4
00:00:16.132 --> 00:00:18.761
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.817
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.683 --> 00:00:24.777
Discourse.

7
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:30.372
Discourse means language in use, especially as it reflects social practices.

8
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:34.214
Political discourse often avoids naming responsibility.

9
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:37.434
Your turn. Say: Discourse.

10
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.417
Discourse.

11
00:00:42.183 --> 00:00:43.045
Register.

12
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:47.640
Register means the variety of language suited to a situation.

13
00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:50.795
The email shifted into an informal register.

14
00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:54.233
Your turn. Say: Register.

15
00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:57.859
Register.

16
00:00:58.617 --> 00:00:59.676
Context.

17
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:03.797
Context means the situation that gives words their meaning.

18
00:01:04.383 --> 00:01:07.033
Out of context, the quotation is misleading.

19
00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:09.886
Your turn. Say: Context.

20
00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:13.792
Context.

21
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.899
Power dynamics.

22
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:21.228
Power dynamics means the way power is distributed and exercised between people.

23
00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:24.699
Interruptions reveal the power dynamics in a meeting.

24
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:28.539
Your turn. Say: Power dynamics.

25
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.749
Power dynamics.

26
00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:34.206
Framing.

27
00:01:35.217 --> 00:01:39.910
Framing means presenting an issue so that a particular interpretation is encouraged.

28
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:43.004
The framing of the story blamed the victims.

29
00:01:43.617 --> 00:01:46.141
Your turn. Say: Framing.

30
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:49.862
Framing.

31
00:01:50.617 --> 00:01:51.850
Implicature.

32
00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.568
Implicature means what is suggested by an utterance without being said.

33
00:01:57.183 --> 00:02:00.669
'Some students passed' carries the implicature that not all did.

34
00:02:01.283 --> 00:02:04.005
Your turn. Say: Implicature.

35
00:02:06.867 --> 00:02:08.112
Implicature.

36
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:09.816
Cohesion.

37
00:02:10.817 --> 00:02:14.151
Cohesion means the grammatical links that hold a text together.

38
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.063
Pronouns and connectors create cohesion.

39
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.838
Your turn. Say: Cohesion.

40
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:23.678
Cohesion.

41
00:02:24.417 --> 00:02:25.766
Genre convention.

42
00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.596
Genre convention means an expected feature of a type of text.

43
00:02:31.183 --> 00:02:34.054
Formal greetings are a genre convention of letters.

44
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.720
Your turn. Say: Genre convention.

45
00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:41.858
Genre convention.

46
00:02:42.667 --> 00:02:46.643
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:51.422
Visal, what did you notice in the two news reports about the protest?

48
00:02:52.517 --> 00:03:00.928
One said 'police injured protesters'; the other said 'protesters were injured'. In the second, the agent was simply removed.

49
00:03:02.017 --> 00:03:04.446
So the passive can hide responsibility.

50
00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:14.356
Yes. Meaning is shaped as much by framing as by the words themselves, and responsibility can be quietly erased.

51
00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.577
Did you find any implicature too?

52
00:03:18.683 --> 00:03:27.184
The second report noted that 'some protesters were peaceful', which implies that most were not, without ever saying so.

53
00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:29.985
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:33.918
Grammar focus: Passive for emphasis on process.

55
00:03:34.218 --> 00:03:35.439
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.729
Meaning is shaped as much by framing as by words themselves.

57
00:03:40.617 --> 00:03:42.767
Protesters were injured during the march.

58
00:03:43.683 --> 00:03:47.099
Responsibility can be erased by a simple change of structure.

59
00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:59.210
The passive puts the process or the affected person in focus and lets the writer omit the agent. Analysts ask: who has been left out, and why? 'As much by X as by Y' adds balance.

60
00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:03.763
Be careful! Don't say: Meaning is shape by framing.

61
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.268
Say: Meaning is shaped by framing.

62
00:04:08.568 --> 00:04:10.940
Passive: be + past participle.

63
00:04:11.733 --> 00:04:14.165
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:14.967 --> 00:04:16.154
Words and Power.

65
00:04:16.454 --> 00:04:21.762
Discourse analysis studies language not as an abstract system but as a social practice.

66
00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:29.178
Analysts examine how choices of vocabulary, grammar and framing position readers to accept particular views.

67
00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:36.079
When an agent is omitted through the passive, responsibility for an action can become strangely invisible.

68
00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:43.060
Implicature works more subtly, since readers infer meanings that the writer never explicitly states.

69
00:04:44.067 --> 00:04:49.491
Recognising these strategies is essential if citizens are to read news and political speeches critically.

70
00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.683
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:55.517 --> 00:04:57.725
Choose the passive that hides the agent.

72
00:04:58.025 --> 00:05:03.699
A, The minister cut the budget.. B, The budget was cut.. C, The minister was cutting the budget..

73
00:05:07.233 --> 00:05:09.929
The answer is B: The budget was cut..

74
00:05:11.083 --> 00:05:16.116
'Some guests enjoyed the food' suggests that others did not. This is an example of blank.

75
00:05:16.416 --> 00:05:20.951
A, implicature. B, cohesion. C, register.

76
00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.685
The answer is A: implicature.

77
00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:31.011
Meaning is shaped as much by framing blank by words.

78
00:05:31.311 --> 00:05:34.047
A, than. B, as. C, like.

79
00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.391
The answer is B: as.

80
00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.641
Pronouns and connectors create blank in a text.

81
00:05:43.941 --> 00:05:47.977
A, framing. B, power dynamics. C, cohesion.

82
00:05:51.533 --> 00:05:53.359
The answer is C: cohesion.

83
00:05:54.517 --> 00:05:58.176
According to the reading, discourse analysis treats language as…

84
00:05:58.476 --> 00:06:04.696
A, an abstract system. B, a social practice. C, a set of grammar rules only.

85
00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:10.837
The answer is B: a social practice.

86
00:06:12.033 --> 00:06:15.162
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:16.017 --> 00:06:18.887
How does the way something is 'framed' change its meaning?

88
00:06:35.683 --> 00:06:37.230
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:37.530 --> 00:07:08.530
Framing changes meaning because it tells us which part of a story is important before we have thought about it. For example, describing a tax as 'relief' suggests that taxes are a burden, while 'investment' suggests a benefit. The facts are identical, yet readers react differently. Some argue that every message is framed, so we cannot avoid it. That is true, which is why I think we should always ask who chose the frame and what it leaves out.

90
00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:10.749
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:16.450
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive for emphasis on process.

92
00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:22.897
Today's value is critical awareness. Use technology wisely, and always think for yourself.

93
00:07:23.197 --> 00:07:25.768
See you in Unit twelve. Goodbye!

94
00:07:26.068 --> 00:07:28.523
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

